WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:07.720
<v Ira Glass>Rebecca remembers exactly when she learned the astonishing truth. She was in second grade and ran into her best friend, Rachel, at school one day.
<v Ira Glass>丽贝卡清楚地记得她是什么时候得知那个惊人真相的。她当时上二年级，有一天在学校遇到了她最好的朋友蕾切尔。

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:29.690
<v Rebecca>And she pulled me aside and said, you know, last night I lost a tooth and I woke up while the tooth fairy was putting the money under my pillow. And guess who the tooth fairy was? I said, oh, my god, who was it? I have to know. And she said, my dad. My dad is the tooth fairy.
<v Rebecca>她把我拉到一边说，你知道吗，昨晚我掉了一颗牙，牙仙把钱塞到我枕头底下时我醒了。你猜牙仙是谁？我说，哦，天哪，是谁？我一定要知道。她说，我爸爸。我爸爸就是牙仙。

00:00:29.690 --> 00:01:24.150
<v Rebecca>And I remember running home after school and telling my mom, Mom, I know who the tooth fairy is and declaring it as if I had grown up, that I knew who the tooth fairy was. And she said, oh, well, who is the tooth fairy? And I turned to her and I said, Rachel's dad is the tooth fairy. Ronnie Loberfeld is the tooth fairy. And she said, I can't believe you know. It's totally secret. You can't let anyone else know. But you're right, Ronnie is the tooth fairy. And he works really hard and it's a secret so you can't let anyone else know. He is the tooth fairy, but you can't let anyone else know. And from that day on, Ronnie Loberfeld was the tooth fairy. And all of my notes under my pillow were signed, Love, Ronnie Loberfeld.
<v Rebecca>好吧，当我学会走动、有了第一辆三轮车时，我可以走得稍微远一点了。所以我冒险走到街上。我抬头看到这对夫妇坐在那儿，这两个人。但他们是我从未见过的人。我从未见过这样的人，因为他们是白人。因为我从未见过白人，我以为他们是鬼魂。

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:28.770
<v Ira Glass>Now in his day job, what did Ronnie Loberfeld do?
<v Ira Glass>那么在日常生活中，罗尼·洛伯费尔德是做什么的呢？

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:54.330
<v Rebecca>I think he did something in finance. He was either an accountant or a stockbroker. He worked next to a Stop and Shop in Massachusetts, in Newton. Had dark hair. Wore a suit. And I definitely had images of his driving his Volvo around the Boston area and delivering the tooth fairy treats.
<v Rebecca>我想他是从事金融工作的。他要么是会计，要么是股票经纪人。他在马萨诸塞州牛顿市的一家“停止与购物”（Stop and Shop）超市旁边工作。留着深色头发。穿着西装。我脑海里绝对有他开着沃尔沃汽车在波士顿地区到处派送牙仙礼物的画面。

00:01:54.330 --> 00:02:11.620
<v Rebecca>I remember wondering what it was like for Rachel to know that her dad was the tooth fairy and definitely being a little envious that her dad had this special job and this special power and that he had this whole other interesting life, where my dad just came home from work and that was it.
<v Rebecca>我记得自己当时很好奇，蕾切尔知道她爸爸是牙仙是一种什么样的体验，而且我绝对有点嫉妒她爸爸有这份特殊的工作、这种特殊的超能力，并且还有着完全不同且有趣的人生——而我的爸爸下班回家后就什么都没有了。

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:17.610
<v Ira Glass>So when you would actually run into Ronnie Loberfeld, what was it like for you? How would you act?
<v Ira Glass>所以当你真的遇到罗尼·洛伯费尔德时，那是什么感觉？你会怎么表现？

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:22.880
<v Rebecca>I tried to act cool. You know, it's like if you're starstruck but you don't want them to know that you're starstruck.
<v Rebecca>我尽量表现得很酷。你知道的，就好像你追星，但又不想让他们知道你在追星一样。

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.360
<v Ira Glass>Just like meeting a celebrity.
<v Ira Glass>就像见到名人一样。

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:35.130
<v Rebecca>Exactly. You downplay it. You try not to mention it, but you definitely check them out twice and look at them when they walk away. Like, oh, my god, you're the tooth fairy.
<v Rebecca>没错。你表现得很低调。你尽量不提这事，但当他们走开时，你绝对会忍不住回头多看两眼，心想：哦，天哪，你就是牙仙。

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.360
<v Ira Glass>But you knew enough to play it cool.
<v Ira Glass>但你还算懂得装酷。

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:47.220
<v Rebecca>I knew enough to play it cool. I said, hey, how you doing? What's for dinner? How am I getting home tonight? Are my parents going to pick me up? Have they called?
<v Rebecca>我挺懂得装酷的。我说，嘿，最近怎么样？今晚吃什么？我今晚怎么回家？我爸妈会来接我吗？他们打电话了吗？

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:52.280
<v Ira Glass>You did play it cool.
<v Ira Glass>你确实装得很酷。

00:02:52.280 --> 00:03:11.490
<v Ira Glass>One interesting question in all of this. Why did both girls come to what seems like the least likely conclusion from the evidence in front of them? Of a parent swapping money for a tooth under a pillow? Well, Alison Gopnik studies how children think. And she says, of course it's logical for a seven-year-old to conclude that her own dad might be the tooth fairy.
<v Ira Glass>这里有一个有趣的问题。为什么两个女孩会从眼前的证据——父母用钱换走枕头底下的牙齿——得出看似最不可能的结论呢？艾莉森·高普尼克（Alison Gopnik）专门研究儿童的思维方式。她说，七岁小孩得出“自己的爸爸可能就是牙仙”这样的结论，当然是很符合逻辑的。

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:42.580
<v Alison Gopnik>Children understand that their parents, for instance, are powerful in all sorts of ways that make them very different from children. Now from a child's point of view, knowing where those powers begin and end is pretty tricky. I mean, think about all the things that your parents can do that you can't do. And think about the fact that there isn't any obvious explanation about why your father can use a Visa card, for instance, which is something that you can't do. The power to be a tooth fairy isn't all that much more impressive.
<v Alison Gopnik>孩子们明白，他们的父母在各种方面都很强大，这让他们与小孩子有很大不同。现在从孩子的角度来看，弄清楚这些超能力的边界在哪里是相当棘手的。我是说，想想所有父母能做到而你做不到的事情。再想想这样一个事实：对于为什么你父亲能使用Visa卡（而这是你做不到的），并没有任何显而易见的解释。拥有牙仙的超能力其实并没有比这更让人印象深刻了。

00:03:42.580 --> 00:04:01.710
<v Ira Glass>There's a certain kind of story that kids tell, like the Ronnie Loberfeld story, where they look at something going on around them, observe it carefully, think about it logically, how one thing connects to the next thing to the next, and then come to conclusions that are completely incorrect. Therapist [? Eileen ?] Goldman in Texas tells this story about a little girl on an airplane.
<v Ira Glass>孩子们会讲一种特定类型的故事，比如罗尼·洛伯费尔德的故事：他们观察周围发生的事情，仔细观察，合乎逻辑地思考事物之间是如何一环扣一环地联系在一起的，然后得出完全错误的结论。德克萨斯的治疗师[？爱琳？]·戈德曼（Eileen Goldman）讲了这样一个关于一个小女孩坐飞机的故事。

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:25.030
<v Eileen Goldman>And she was about four years old and her very first flight. And as the plane was airborne, she turned to the woman next to her and said, when do we get smaller? That had been her experience at airports watching airplanes take off. They do get smaller.
<v Eileen Goldman>她大约四岁，那是她第一次坐飞机。当飞机在空中飞行时，她转过身对身边的女人说：“我们什么时候变小？”这源于她在机场观看飞机起飞的体验。它们确实变小了。

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:46.490
<v Ira Glass>These stories are like jokes, and they're also like poems, I think because there's this aha quality to them, some connection that's made between things, a surprising connection, a wrong connection, actually. Well, we at This American Life love these stories. And so today, we bring you a full hour of them. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这些故事就像笑话，我觉得它们也像诗歌，因为它们有一种“醍醐灌顶”的特质，事物之间建立起某种联系——一种令人惊讶的联系，其实是一种错误的联系。好吧，我们《This American Life》非常喜欢这些故事。所以今天，我们为你带来整整一小时这样的故事。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这里是《This American Life》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:04:46.490 --> 00:05:17.930
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, kid logic. Our show in four acts. Act 1, baby scientists with faulty data. Act 2, werewolves in their youth. That story from Michael Chabon. Act 3, the game ain't over till the fatso man sings. Act 4, when small thoughts meet big brains. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目的主题是：儿童逻辑。我们的节目分为四个部分。第一幕：拥有错误数据的婴儿科学家。第二幕：少年时期的狼人。这个故事来自迈克尔·谢蓬（Michael Chabon）。第三幕：胖子没唱歌，比赛就没结束。第四幕：当小小的想法遇到伟大的大脑。请继续收听。

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:51.270
<v Ira Glass>Baby scientists with faulty data. 30 years ago, psychologists and scientists believed that babies could not think at all, that they were irrational and illogical, self-centered little balls of need and want. And what science has learned in the last 30 years is that this is not true, that children are observing the world and thinking about it and coming to logical conclusions from the day they're born. When Alison Gopnik and two of her colleagues decided to summarize a lot of this research in a book, they called it The Scientist in the Crib, meaning that babies are like little scientists.
<v Ira Glass>拥有错误数据的婴儿科学家。30年前，心理学家和科学家认为婴儿根本不会思考，他们是不理性的、不讲逻辑的、以自我为中心的、充满需求和欲望的小肉球。而科学在过去30年里了解到的是，事实并非如此，孩子们从出生那一天起就在观察世界、思考世界，并得出合乎逻辑的结论。当艾莉森·高普尼克和她的两位同事决定把大量这类研究总结成一本书时，他们将其命名为《摇篮里的科学家》，意思是婴儿就像小科学家一样。

00:05:51.270 --> 00:06:02.910
<v Ira Glass>They argue that when a small baby sits in a high chair and drops a spoon onto the floor over and over and over for Mom or Dad to pick up, what the baby is doing, essentially, is running a little baby-sized experiment.
<v Ira Glass>他们认为，当一个小婴儿坐在高脚椅上，一遍又一遍地把勺子扔到地板上，让爸爸或妈妈去捡时，婴儿本质上在做的事情，就是一个婴儿体量的小实验。

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:23.340
<v Alison Gopnik>Because it turns out that babies are very interested in gravity and how gravity works. The fact that things fall down and not up is not obvious to babies. And it turns out another thing they're very interested in is human beings and how they work. We are actually the lab rats. They're actually doing experiments on us to see how we tick.
<v Alison Gopnik>因为事实证明，婴儿对重力以及重力是如何运作的非常感兴趣。东西会往下落而不会往上飞，这对婴儿来说并不是显而易见的。事实证明，他们非常感兴趣的另一件事是人类及其运作方式。我们实际上就是小白鼠。他们实际上在拿我们做实验，以摸清我们的底细。

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:40.440
<v Alison Gopnik>So when you play drop the spoon, you get two for the price of one. You get an experiment about gravity. You get a little physics tutorial. And you get a psychology tutorial. You can see about how that person will do something over and over again.
<v Alison Gopnik>所以当你玩“扔勺子”游戏时，你花一份钱能得到两份收获。你得到了一个关于重力的实验。你得到了一个小型物理学教程。你还得到了一个心理学教程。你可以观察那个人是如何一遍又一遍地做同一件事的。

00:06:40.440 --> 00:07:00.350
<v Ira Glass>Well, kids think with the same logic that adults use and apply that logic just as rigorously. There are certain things that they simply do not know and take a while to figure out. Up to six or seven years old, for instance, it's not exactly clear to anyone what is imaginary and what is not, or if wishing for something can make it come true.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，孩子们的思考逻辑与成年人使用的逻辑相同，并且同样严格地应用这种逻辑。有些事情他们根本不知道，需要花一段时间才能弄明白。例如，直到六七岁时，对于什么是想象出来的、什么不是，或者许愿能否让事情成真，任何人其实都还没完全弄清楚。

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:28.110
<v Alison Gopnik>There's a wonderful experiment about this, actually, that Paul Harris in England did, where he got children to imagine that something was in a box. So he would say, OK, now here's this box. We're going to open it up. We're going to close it. Now let's imagine that there's a puppy in this box, or else let's imagine that there's a monster in the box. And he asked the children, is there really a monster in the box? Is there really a puppy in the box? They said no. They were just imagining it.
<v Alison Gopnik>实际上有一个关于这方面的精彩实验，是英国的保罗·哈里斯（Paul Harris）做的。他让孩子们想象盒子里有什么东西。所以他会说：“好了，现在这是个我们要打开的盒子。我们要把它关上。现在让我们想象盒子里有一只小狗，或者让我们想象盒子里有一只怪物。”他问孩子们：“盒子里真的有怪物吗？盒子里真的有小狗吗？”他们回答说没有。他们只是想象出来的。

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:47.410
<v Ira Glass>Then the researcher would walk out of the room, leaving the box behind with the child. And then something funny would happen. The kids who were told to imagine a puppy in the box would go over and peek inside the box, just to check. And the kids who were told that there was a monster in the box? They would edge away from the box.
<v Ira Glass>然后研究人员会走出房间，把盒子留在孩子身边。接着有趣的事情发生了。被告知盒子里有一只小狗的孩子会走过去，朝盒子里偷看，只是为了确认一下。而那些被告知盒子里有怪物的孩子呢？他们会挪开身体，离盒子远远的。

00:07:47.410 --> 00:08:08.590
<v Alison Gopnik>So they weren't going to take any chances, just in case wishing actually could make monsters happen. They didn't want to take any chances about what was going on in that box. But by the time the children are six or seven, like grownups, they've understood that just wishing for things isn't going to actually make them happen.
<v Alison Gopnik>所以他们绝不冒任何风险，以防万一许愿真的能让怪物成真。他们不想对盒子里发生的事情冒任何险。但等到孩子们长到六七岁时，就像成年人一样，他们已经明白，仅仅祈求某事发生并不能真正让它实现。

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:24.010
<v Ira Glass>When they're still small and inexperienced about what happens in the real world, children have to make logical inferences all the time based on the data that they do have. Here is how children responded when our producer, Jonathan Goldstein, asked them about the tooth fairy.
<v Ira Glass>当孩子们还很小、对真实世界中发生的事情缺乏经验时，他们必须时刻根据自己手头拥有的数据做出合乎逻辑的推论。以下是我们的制作人乔纳森·戈德斯坦（Jonathan Goldstein）向孩子们询问关于牙仙时他们的回答。

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:28.230
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What do you think she does with all of these teeth that she's collecting?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你觉得她收集了这么多牙齿都用来做什么？

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:30.730
<v Girl>Maybe she gives it to the people without teeth.
<v Girl>也许她把它们送给没有牙齿的人。

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.200
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Like who?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>比如谁？

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:33.790
<v Girl>Old people.
<v Girl>老人。

00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:36.400
<v Jonathan Goldstein>What do you think she does with all these teeth?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你觉得她收集这些牙齿是用来干嘛的？

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.210
<v Boy>I really think she just likes to collect teeth and make things out of them.
<v Boy>我真的觉得她只是喜欢收集牙齿，然后用它们做东西。

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.010
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Like what kind of stuff?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>做什么类型的东西？

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:51.310
<v Boy>Lots of stuff. Makes a tooth house, tooth trophy, and a tooth desk.
<v Boy>很多东西。做一座牙齿房子、一个牙齿奖杯和一个牙齿书桌。

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:54.300
<v Jonathan Goldstein>How many teeth do you think it takes to make a tooth house?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>你觉得造一座牙齿房子需要多少颗牙齿？

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.530
<v Boy>100. 100.
<v Boy>100. 100.

00:08:57.530 --> 00:09:01.560
<v Jonathan Goldstein>Why wouldn't she just make the house out of bricks like everyone else?
<v Jonathan Goldstein>她为什么不干脆像其他人一样用砖头盖房子呢？

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:14.920
<v Girl>Because I don't-- because no one doesn't have brick teeth.
<v Girl>因为我不知道——因为没有人有砖头牙齿。

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:36.880
<v Ira Glass>These stories, where kids take a perfectly logical premise and go through a series of perfectly logical deductions that lead to perfectly incorrect conclusions, it turns out that science does not have a name for these stories, which is surprising, given how common they are and how they are recognized around the world for their sheer entertainment value. Here we've collected a few more.
<v Ira Glass>这些故事——孩子们接受一个完全合乎逻辑的前提，然后经过一系列完全合乎逻辑的推导，最终得出完全错误的结论——事实证明，科学界对这类故事还没有一个专门的命名。考虑到它们是如此常见，并且因其纯粹的娱乐价值而在世界各地广受认可，这着实令人惊讶。在这里，我们又收集了一些。

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:51.580
<v Gianofer Fields>We lived in a duplex. The duplexes directly to the left and the right of us were aunts and uncles. Across the street from us, all aunts and uncles. So there was no such thing as walking out and seeing a stranger. I just thought we all looked alike. We all had common ancestries.
<v Gianofer Fields>我们住在一栋复式公寓里。紧挨着我们左右两边的复式公寓住的是叔叔阿姨。我们街对面，全都是叔叔阿姨。所以根本不可能走出门看到陌生人。我只是觉得我们长得都一样。我们都有共同的祖先。

00:09:51.580 --> 00:10:14.500
<v Gianofer Fields>Well, when I became mobile, when I got my first tricycle, I could go a little bit further. So I ventured down the street. And I looked and I saw this couple sitting there, these two people. But they were people that I had never seen before. I had never seen anything like that because they were white people. And because I had never seen white people, I assumed that they were ghosts.
<v Gianofer Fields>嗯，当我能活动了，当我有了第一辆三轮车时，我可以走得远一点了。于是我顺着街道走了下去。然后我看到有一对夫妇坐在那里，这两个人。但他们是我从未见过的人。我从未见过那样的人，因为他们是白人。因为我从未见过白人，我以为他们是鬼魂。

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:35.740
<v Gianofer Fields>So I waved, like, I wonder if I wave, what kind of people are they? What do they do? Do they talk? So I waved, and I remember hearing the man going [COUGHING] And I thought, wow, that must be the way they talk.
<v Gianofer Fields>所以我挥了挥手，心想，如果我挥手会怎么样，他们是什么样的人？他们做些什么？他们会说话吗？所以我挥了挥手，我记得听到那个男的发出了“（咳嗽声）”的声音。我想，哇，那一定就是他们说话的方式。

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:50.900
<v Gianofer Fields>It was like a scientific discovery. I discovered the first ghost people, and they talked to me. I communicated. I waved. They waved. I said hello, and they said hello in their language. [COUGHING]
<v Gianofer Fields>这就像是一个科学发现。我发现了第一批鬼魂，而且他们跟我说话了。我进行了交流。我挥手。他们挥手。我说了你好，他们用他们的语言说了你好。“（咳嗽声）”

00:10:50.900 --> 00:11:23.350
<v Jack Hitt>Well, it all began at Christmas two years ago, when my daughter was four years old. And it was the first time that she had ever asked about what did this holiday mean. And so I explained to her that this was celebrating the birth of Jesus. And she wanted to know more about that. And we went out and bought a kid's Bible and had these readings at night. She loved them, wanted to know everything about Jesus.
<v Jack Hitt>这一切始于两年前的圣诞节，当时我女儿四岁。那是她第一次询问这个节日意味着什么。所以我向她解释说，这是为了庆祝耶稣的诞生。她想了解更多这方面的知识。我们出去买了一本儿童圣经，晚上读这些内容。她很喜欢，想了解关于耶稣的一切。

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:43.340
<v Jack Hitt>So we read a lot about his birth and about his teaching, and she would ask constantly what that phrase was. And I would explain to her that it was "Do unto others as you would have them do unto you." And we would talk about those old words and what that all meant, you know?
<v Jack Hitt>所以我们读了很多关于他的诞生和他的教导的内容，她会不断地问那句话是什么。我会向她解释那是“你们愿意别人怎样对待你们，你们也要怎样对待别人”。我们会讨论那些古老的词汇以及这一切到底意味着什么，你知道的？

00:11:43.340 --> 00:12:28.810
<v Jack Hitt>And then one day, we were driving past a big church, and out front was an enormous crucifix. She said, "Who is that?" And I guess I'd never really told that part of the story. So I had this sort of "Yeah, oh, well, that's Jesus. And I forgot to tell you the ending. Yeah, well, he ran afoul of the Roman government." This message that he had was so radical and unnerving to the prevailing authorities of the time that they had to kill him. They came to the conclusion that he would have to die. That message was too troublesome.
<v Jack Hitt>然后有一天，我们开车路过一座大教堂，正前方是一个巨大的耶稣受难像。她问：“那是谁？”我想我以前从来没有真正讲过故事的那个部分。所以我当时的心情大概是：“哦，好吧，那是耶稣。我忘了告诉你结局了。嗯，好吧，他和罗马政府起了冲突。”他传达的信息对当时当权的当权者来说是如此激进和令人不安，以至于他们不得不杀了他。他们得出的结论是，他必须死。这个信息太令人头疼了。

00:12:28.810 --> 00:13:09.700
<v Jack Hitt>It was about a month later after that Christmas. We'd gone through the whole story of what Christmas meant, and it was mid-January. And her preschool celebrates the same holidays as the local schools. So Martin Luther King Day was off. So I knocked off work that day, and I decided we'd play and I'd take her out to lunch. And we were sitting in there, and right on the table where we happened to plop down was the art section of the local newspaper. And there, big as life, was a huge drawing by a 10-year-old kid in the local schools of Martin Luther King.
<v Jack Hitt>那大约是在那个圣诞节后一个月。我们已经过完了圣诞节所代表的全部含义，当时是1月中旬。她的幼儿园和当地学校放假时间一样，所以马丁·路德·金纪念日放假。所以我那天请了假，决定我们一起玩，我带她出去吃午饭。我们正坐在那里，刚好摆在我们面前的桌子上有当地报纸的艺术版。在那里，画着一幅当地学校一个10岁小孩画的马丁·路德·金的巨幅画像，活灵活现。

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:43.530
<v Jack Hitt>And she said, "Who's that?" And I said, "Well, as it happens, that's Martin Luther King. And he's why you're not in school today. So we're celebrating his birthday. This is the day we celebrate his life." And she said, "So who was he?" And I said, "Well, he was a preacher." And she looks up at me and goes, "For Jesus?" And I said, "Yeah. Yeah, actually, he was. But there was another thing that he was really famous for, which is that he had a message."
<v Jack Hitt>她说：“那是谁？”我说：“嗯，碰巧的是，那是马丁·路德·金。就是因为他，你今天才不用上学。所以我们在庆祝他的生日。这是我们庆祝他一生的日子。”她说：“那他是谁？”我说：“嗯，他是个牧师。”她抬起头看着我问：“为耶稣吗？”我说：“对。对，实际上他确实是。但还有一件事他非常有名，那就是他有一个讯息。”

00:13:43.530 --> 00:14:11.630
<v Jack Hitt>And you're trying to say this to a four-year-old. This is the first time they ever hear anything, so you're just very careful about how you phrase everything. So I said, "Well, yeah, he was a preacher and he had a message." She said, "What was his message?" And I said, "Well, he said that you should treat everybody the same no matter what they look like."
<v Jack Hitt>你正试图对一个四岁的小孩说这些。这是他们第一次听到任何事情，所以你对如何措辞所有事情都非常小心。所以我说：“嗯，对，他是个牧师，他有一个讯息。”她说：“他的讯息是什么？”我说：“嗯，他说过，不管别人长什么样，你应该一视同仁地对待所有人。”

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:48.220
<v Jack Hitt>And she thought about that for a minute. And she said, "Well, that's what Jesus said." And I said, "Yeah, I guess it is. I never thought of it that way, but yeah." And that is sort of like, "Do unto others as you would have them do unto you." And she thought for a minute and looked up at me and said, "Did they kill him too?"
<v Jack Hitt>她思考了一会儿。然后她说：“嗯，耶稣也是这么说的。”我说：“对，我想是的。我从来没从这个角度想过，但确实是。”这有点像是“你们愿意别人怎样对待你们，你们也要怎样对待别人”。她想了一会儿，抬头看着我问：“他们也把他杀了吗？”

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:57.450
<v Ira Glass>Act 2, "Werewolves in their Youth." In this act, we have this example of kids thinking like kids. It's an excerpt of a short story by Michael Chabon.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“少年时期的狼人”。在这幕中，我们有这样一个孩子们像孩子一样思考的例子。这是迈克尔·谢蓬（Michael Chabon）一篇短篇小说的摘录。

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:18.270
<v Michael Chabon>I had known him as a bulldozer, as a samurai, as an android programmed to kill, as Plastic Man and Titanium Man and Matter Eater Lad, as a Buick Electra, as a peterbilt truck, and even for a week as the Mackinaw Bridge. But it was as a werewolf that Timothy Stokes finally went too far.
<v Michael Chabon>我以前认识他时，他是一台推土机、一个武士、一个被编程杀戮的安卓机器人、塑料人（Plastic Man）、钛金人（Titanium Man）和食质小子（Matter Eater Lad）、一辆别克埃莱克特拉（Buick Electra）、一辆彼得比尔特（Peterbilt）卡车，甚至有一周他还当过麦基诺大桥（Mackinaw Bridge）。但最终，蒂莫西·斯托克斯（Timothy Stokes）是作为一只狼人彻底玩过头了。

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:38.400
<v Michael Chabon>I wasn't there when it happened. I was down in the ravine at the edge of the schoolyard, founding a capital for an empire of ants. I had just begun to describe to myself and to the ants the complicated rites sacred to the god whose worship I was imposing on them when I heard the first screams from the playground.
<v Michael Chabon>事情发生时我不在场。我在校场边缘的沟壑里，为蚂蚁帝国奠定都城。我刚开始向自己和蚂蚁们描述一场复杂仪式的神圣仪式，这个神的神明崇拜是我强加给它们的，这时我听到了操场上第一声尖叫。

00:15:38.400 --> 00:16:06.120
<v Michael Chabon>The girls screamed at Timothy the same way every time he came after them, in unison, and with a trill that sounded almost like delight, as if they were watching the family cat trot past with something bloody in its jaws. I scrambled up the side of the ravine and emerged as Timothy, shoulders hunched, arms outstretched, growled realistically and declared that he was hungry for the throats of puny humans.
<v Michael Chabon>每当蒂莫西追赶她们时，女孩们总会异口同声地尖叫，带着一种听起来几乎像是欢快颤音的腔调，仿佛她们在看着家里的猫小跑着走过，嘴里还叼着血淋淋的东西。我爬上沟壑的斜坡，探出头时，蒂莫西正耸着肩膀，伸开双臂，逼真地低吼着，宣称他渴望吃掉孱弱人类的喉咙。

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:28.680
<v Michael Chabon>Timothy said this or something like it every time he turned into a werewolf, and I would not have been too concerned if, in the course of his last transformation he hadn't actually gone and bitten Virginia Bease on the neck. It was common knowledge around school that Virginia's parents had since written a letter to the principal, and that the next time Timothy Stokes hurt somebody he was going to be expelled.
<v Michael Chabon>蒂莫西每次变成狼人时都会说这样或类似的话，如果不是在他上一次变身的过程中，他真的去咬了弗吉尼亚·比斯的脖子，我其实并不会太担心。学校里人尽皆知，从那以后，弗吉尼亚的父母给校长写了一信，而且蒂莫西·斯托克斯下次再伤害别人时就会被开除。

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:51.190
<v Michael Chabon>Timothy was, in our teacher Mrs. Gladfelter's words, "one strike away from an out." And there was a widespread, unarticulated hope among his classmates, their parents, and all of the teachers at Copeland Fork Elementary that one day soon, he would provide the authorities with the excuse they needed to pack him off to special school.
<v Michael Chabon>用我们的老师格拉德费尔特太太的话说，蒂莫西是“只差一个好球就要三振出局了”。科普兰福克小学的同学、他们的家长以及所有老师心中，都有着一种普遍而又心照不宣的希望：那就是总有一天，他会给当局提供他们所需的借口，把他打发到特殊学校去。

00:16:51.190 --> 00:17:21.490
<v Michael Chabon>I stood there a while above my little city, watching Timothy pursue a snarling, lupine course along the hopscotch crosses. I knew that someone ought to do something to calm him down, but I was the only one in our school who could have any reason to want to save Timothy Stokes from expulsion. And I hated him with all my heart.
<v Michael Chabon>我在我那座小小的城市上方站了一会儿，看着蒂莫西沿着跳房子的格子发出咆哮，迈着狼一般步伐追逐。我知道应该有人做点什么来让他平静下来，但我是我们学校里唯一一个可能会有理由想要拯救蒂莫西·斯托克斯免于被开除的人。而我打心底里恨他。

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:48.940
<v Michael Chabon>"I've been cursed for 300 years," he declaimed. He was wearing a standard uniform of white dungarees and a plain white undershirt, even though it was a chilly afternoon in October and all the rest of us had long since been bundled up for autumn in corduroy and down. "I have been cursed to stalk the night through all eternity," he went on. "I've been searching for prey as lovely as you!"
<v Michael Chabon>"我已经受了三百年的诅咒，"他大声朗诵道。他穿着标准的白色斜纹棉裤和一件朴素的白色内衣，尽管这是一个十月寒冷的下午，我们其余的人早就穿上了灯芯绒和羽绒服等厚实的秋装。"我被诅咒在永恒岁月中潜行于黑夜，"他继续说道。"我一直在寻找像你们这样迷人的猎物！"

00:17:48.940 --> 00:18:17.410
<v Michael Chabon>He lunged toward the nearest wall of the cage of girls around him. The girls peeled away from him, as though sprayed with a hose, bumped shoulders, clung shrieking to each other's sleeves. Some of them were singing the song we sang about Timothy Stokes. "Timothy Stokes, Timothy Stokes, you're going to the home for crazy folks." And the one singing the loudest was Virginia Bease herself, in her furry black coat and her bright red tights.
<v Michael Chabon>他猛地扑向周围女孩形成的牢笼最近的墙壁。女孩们像被水管喷到一样从他身边散开，互相碰撞着肩膀，尖叫着抓紧彼此的袖子。其中一些人唱起了我们编的关于蒂莫西·斯托克斯的歌。"蒂莫西·斯托克斯，蒂莫西·斯托克斯，你要去疯人收容所啦。"其中唱得最大声的正是弗吉尼亚·比斯本人，她穿着毛茸茸的黑色外套和鲜红色的紧身裤。

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:36.550
<v Michael Chabon>Virginia had blond hair, and she was the only girl in the fifth grade with pierced ears and painted fingernails. And Timothy Stokes was in love with her. I knew this, because the Stokeses lived next door to us and I was privy to all kinds of secrets about Timothy that had absolutely no desire to know.
<v Michael Chabon>弗吉尼亚有一头金发，她是五年级里唯一一个打耳洞和涂指甲油的女孩。而蒂莫西·斯托克斯暗恋着她。我知道这一点，因为斯托克斯家就住在我们隔壁，我对蒂莫西各种各样的秘密了如指掌，而我其实根本不想知道这些。

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:54.250
<v Michael Chabon>I forbade myself, with an almost religious severity, to show Timothy any kindness or regard. I would never let him sit beside me at lunch or in class. And if he tried to talk to me on the playground, I ignored him. It was bad enough that I had to live next door to him.
<v Michael Chabon>我以一种近乎宗教般的严厉禁止自己对蒂莫西表现出任何友善或关照。我绝不让他午餐时或上课时坐在我旁边。如果他在操场上试图跟我说话，我就不理他。我不得不和他住在隔壁已经够糟糕的了。

00:18:54.250 --> 00:19:24.900
<v Michael Chabon>It was toward Virginia that Timothy now advanced, a rattling growl in his throat. She drew back behind her girlfriends, and their screaming now grew less melodious, less purely formal. Timothy crouched down on all fours. He rolled his wild white eyes and took a last look around him. That was when he saw me, halfway across the yellow distance of the soccer field. He was looking at me, I thought, as though he hoped I might have something I wanted to tell him.
<v Michael Chabon>蒂莫西现在正发出嘎嘎作响的低吼声，朝着弗吉尼亚逼近。她退到了女友们身后，她们的尖叫声此刻变得不再那么悦耳，不再那么纯粹具有形式感。蒂莫西四肢着地蹲伏下来。他转动着狂野发白双眼，最后环顾了一下四周。就在那时，他看到了我，正处在足球场那片黄色距离的半途。我想，他看着我的眼神，就好像他希望我有什么话想对他说一样。

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:51.740
<v Michael Chabon>Instantly, I dropped flat on my belly, my heart pounding the way it did when I was spotted trying to spy on a baseball game or a birthday party. I slid down into the ravine backward. At first, I could hear the girls shouting for Mrs. Gladfelter. And then I heard Mrs. Gladfelter herself sounding very angry. And the bell sounded the end of recess and everything got very quiet, but I just stayed there in the ravine.
<v Michael Chabon>我立刻趴倒在肚子上，心怦怦直跳，就像我当初被发现试图偷窥棒球比赛或生日派对时的感觉一样。我倒着滑进沟壑里。起初，我能听到女孩们大喊着找格拉德费尔特太太。接着我听到格拉德费尔特太太本人听起来非常生气。然后下课铃响了，一切都变得非常安静，但我只是留在那条沟壑里。

00:19:51.740 --> 00:20:14.360
<v Michael Chabon>I told myself that I didn't feel sorry at all for stupid old Timothy Stokes. But then I would remember the confused look in his eyes as I had abandoned him to his fate, to all the unimaginable things that would be done to him in the fabulous corridors of the special school. I kept recalling something that I had heard Timothy's mother say to mine just a couple of days earlier.
<v Michael Chabon>我告诉自己，我对愚蠢的老蒂莫西·斯托克斯一点也不感到同情。但随后我就会想起我将他抛弃在命运中时他眼中迷茫的神情，想起他在特殊学校那富丽堂皇的走廊里将要遭遇的所有无法想象的事情。我不断想起几天前我听到蒂莫西的母亲对我母亲说的一句话。

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:46.730
<v Michael Chabon>"You know," Althea Stokes had told my mother, in that big, sad, donkey voice of hers, "Your little Paul is Timothy's only friend." I decided to spend the afternoon in the ravine. The sun started down behind the embankment, and the moon, rising early, emerged from the rooftops of the houses somebody was putting up in front of the school. The moon, I noticed, was not quite full.
<v Michael Chabon>"你知道吗，"阿尔西娅·斯托克斯用她那沙哑而悲伤的声音对母亲说，"你们家小保罗是蒂莫西唯一的吸朋友。"我决定在沟壑里度过这个下午。太阳开始落向路基后面，月亮升得很早，从某人在学校前面正在建造的房屋屋顶后露了出来。我注意到，月亮还不够圆。

00:20:46.730 --> 00:21:07.520
<v Michael Chabon>I didn't hear the scrape of footsteps until they were just above my head. "Paul," said Mrs. Gladfelter, leaning over the lip of the ravine, hands against her thighs. "Paul Covell, what on earth are you doing out here?" "Nothing," I said. "I didn't hear the bell." "Paul," she said. "Now, listen to me Paul. I need your help."
<v Michael Chabon>我没有听到脚步声，直到它们就在我的头顶上方。"保罗，"格拉德费尔特太太说，双手叉腰，探身俯视着沟壑边缘。"保罗·科维尔，你到底在这儿干什么？""没什么，"我说。"我没听到铃声。""保罗，"她说。"现在，听我说，保罗。我需要你的帮助。"

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:29.550
<v Michael Chabon>"With what?" I didn't think she looked angry, but her face was upside down and it was hard to tell. "With Timothy, Paul. I guess he's just very wound up right now. He's pretending he's a werewolf today, and even though that's fine and we all know how Timothy is sometimes, we have serious things to discuss with him, and we'd like him to stop pretending for just a little while."
<v Michael Chabon>"帮什么？"我觉得她看起来不像是生气的样子，但她的脸是倒过来的，很难看出来。"关于蒂莫西，保罗。我想他现在情绪非常激动。他今天在假装自己是狼人，尽管这没关系，我们也都知道蒂莫西有时是什么样，但我们有重要的事情要跟他谈谈，我们希望他能暂时停止假装。"

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:51.110
<v Michael Chabon>"What if he isn't pretending?" I said. "What if he really is a werewolf?" "Maybe he is, Paul, but if you would just come inside and talk to him for a little bit, I think we might be able to persuade him to change back into Timothy. You're his friend, Paul. I asked him if he'd like to talk to you and he said yes." "I'm not his friend, Mrs. Gladfelter. I swear to God, I can't do anything."
<v Michael Chabon>"如果他不是在假装呢？"我说。"如果他真的是个狼人呢？""也许他是，保罗，但如果你肯进来跟他聊一会儿，我想我们也许能说服他变回蒂莫西。你是他的朋友，保罗。我问过他是否愿意跟你谈谈，他说好。""我不是他的朋友，格拉德费尔特太太。我向上帝发誓，我什么也做不了。"

00:21:51.110 --> 00:22:06.350
<v Michael Chabon>"Paul, Timothy is in trouble. He needs your help, and I need your help too. Now, if you come right this minute and get up out of that dirt, then I'll forget that you didn't come in from recess. If you don't come back inside, I'll have to speak to your mother."
<v Michael Chabon>"保罗，蒂莫西有麻烦了。他需要你的帮助，我也需要你的帮助。现在，如果你马上过来从那个泥坑里爬出来，那我就当没看见你没从操场进来。如果你不回里面去，我就不得不去找你母亲谈谈了。"

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:25.060
<v Michael Chabon>She held out her hand. "Now, come on, Paul. Please." So I took her hand and let her pull me out of the ravine and across the deserted playground, aware that in doing so I was merely proving the unspoken corollary that my mother had left hanging the other morning in the air between her and Mrs. Stokes.
<v Michael Chabon>她伸出手。"好了，过来，保罗。求你了。"于是我握住她的手，任由她把我拉出沟壑，穿过荒废的操场，我意识到这样做只是印证了那天早上我母亲留在她和斯托克斯太太之间空气中的那个未说出口的推论。

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:59.440
<v Michael Chabon>There was a song about me too, I'm afraid, a popular little number that went, "What's that smello? Paul Covello. He's a big, fat, hippo jell-o. He's a snoop. He smells like poop. He smells like tomato beef alphabet soup." Timothy Stokes, I knew, as I followed Mrs. Gladfelter down the long, silent hallway to the office, hating him more and more with each step, was my only friend.
<v Michael Chabon>恐怕也有首歌是唱我的，这是一个很受欢迎的小曲调，歌词是：“什么东西臭烘烘？保罗·科维洛。他是一大块肥胖的果冻。他是个爱打听的人。他闻起来像便便。他闻起来像西红柿牛肉字母汤。”我知道，当我跟着格拉德费尔特太太走过漫长而沉默的走廊去办公室时，每走一步我对蒂莫西的恨意就加深一层，他是我的唯一的朋友。

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:14.740
<v Michael Chabon>Timothy was sitting in a corner of the office, trapped in an orange vinyl arm chair. There was a Roman numeral three scratched into his left cheek, and his brilliant white shirt and trousers were patterned with a camouflage of grass, and dirt, and asphalt.
<v Michael Chabon>蒂莫西正坐在办公室的角落里，陷在一张橙色乙烯基扶手椅里。他的左脸颊上有一道被抓出的罗马数字三，他那件洁白的衬衣和裤子上沾满了由青草、泥土和柏油交织成的迷彩图案。

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:46.670
<v Michael Chabon>"Well, now, Timothy," Mrs. Gladfelter took me by the shoulders and maneuvered me around her. "Look who I found." "Hey, Timothy," I said. Timothy didn't look up. Mrs. Gladfelter gave me a gentle push toward him in the small of my back. "Why don't you sit down, Paul?" "No!" I didn't want to be left alone with Timothy. Not because I was afraid of him, but because I was afraid that somebody would come into the office and see us sitting there, two matching rejects in matching orange chairs.
