<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Kid Logic. We wanted an hour filled with stories in which kids employ kid thinking, especially the kid thinking that is perfectly logical but completely wrongheaded. And we've arrived at Act 3 of our program. Act 3, "The Game Ain't Over till the Fatso Man Sings."
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们的节目都会选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的不同类型的故事。今天的节目是“儿童逻辑”。我们想要一个充满故事的一小时，在这些故事中，孩子们运用儿童思维，尤其是那种完美符合逻辑但却完全错得离谱的儿童思维。现在我们来到了节目的第三幕。第三幕，“胖子没开腔，比赛不算完。”

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:55.660
<v Ira Glass>When little kids talk about a crush or love, are they talking about more or less the same thing that adults mean by those words? Howie Chackowicz remembers how he thought about love in grade school. He wanted girls to like him, but they never seemed to. Here he is.
<v Ira Glass>当小孩子谈论暗恋或爱情时，他们说的是不是和成年人所指的差不多？豪伊·查科维茨（Howie Chackowicz）还记得他在小学时是如何看待爱情的。他希望女孩子喜欢他，但她们似乎从来没有过。这就是他。

00:31:55.660 --> 00:32:21.900
<v Howie Chackowicz>Looking back on it, I think part of the problem was how I thought about love as a kid. I had a few ideas about how you get someone to love you that, in retrospect, weren't particularly helpful to me. First, I thought that if they could see me sleeping, they would immediately fall for me. When I went to sleep each night, I would consciously try to sleep in a cute way, just in case the girls would peep on me. I'd roll into a fetal ball a kitten and scrunch my head into my pillow, hands under my head.
<v Howie Chackowicz>回想起来，我觉得问题的一部分在于我小时候对爱情的看法。我当时对如何让别人爱上有几种想法，现在回想起来，对我并没有什么帮助。首先，我认为如果她们能看到我睡觉，就会立刻爱上我。每天晚上睡觉时，我都会刻意以一种可爱的方式入睡，以防女孩子偷看我。我会把自己蜷成小猫般的胎儿状，把头埋进枕头里，双手垫在头下面。

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:50.140
<v Howie Chackowicz>I imagined that all the popular girls, intent on cruelly pranking me, on a ladder and climbed up to my bedroom window. But instead of painting fatso or whatever on my window as planned, their collective hearts would melt as they saw me sleeping like a babe, an angel, buried snugly under my blankets. I guess there was some crossover of a kid's knowledge of what was endearing to adults applied to romance.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我幻想着所有那些热衷于对我进行残酷恶作剧的受欢迎的女孩们，架起梯子爬上了我的卧室窗户。但她们没有像计划那样在我的窗户上画“胖子”之类的字眼，而是看到我像个婴儿、一个天使般安睡在厚厚的毯子下，集体心都融化了。我想，这其中大概包含了一种将小孩子对成年人觉得可爱的事物的理解应用到浪漫之中的交叉认知。

00:32:50.140 --> 00:33:04.480
<v Howie Chackowicz>My second theory was that they'd fall in love with me if they can see me reading aloud. This conclusion came out of my experience with nieces and nephews, who'd fawn all over me when I would read to them. By age 6, I was already an uncle, and I felt this lent me a certain maturity.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我的第二个理论是，如果她们能看到我大声朗读，就会爱上我。这个结论来自于我和侄子侄女相处的经验，当我给他们读书时，他们总是对我很崇拜。到6岁时，我已经是叔叔了，我觉得这让我有一种成熟感。

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:23.740
<v Howie Chackowicz>Often, at recess time, I'd go to the back of the classroom and read from a selection of kids books. All the kids would gather around in a circle and I'd pore through books like "Percy the Rose-eating Donkey," effecting the voices of the different characters and speaking with a preacher's sweaty charisma. I'm not sure why, but everyone in my class seemed to love the way I hammed it up.
<v Howie Chackowicz>通常在课间休息时，我会走到教室后面，从精选的儿童书中挑一本读起来。所有的孩子都会围成一圈，我则如痴如醉地读着诸如《吃玫瑰花的驴子帕西》之类的书，模仿不同角色的声音，用布道者那种大汗淋漓的个人魅力进行讲述。我不知道为什么，但班上的每个人似乎都很喜欢我这种夸张的表演。

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:43.540
<v Howie Chackowicz>The only problem with this was, the girls in class ended up treating me like their uncle. They'd call me Uncle Howie and talk to me in baby talk. "Wead me a story, Uncle Howie." And so on. Don't get me wrong. I loved the attention, but I wanted love, not wuv.
<v Howie Chackowicz>唯一的问题是，班上的女孩们最后把我当成她们的叔叔来对待。她们会叫我豪伊叔叔，并用娃娃音跟我说话。“给我读个故事吧，豪伊叔叔。”诸如此类。别误会我。我喜欢这种关注，但我想要的是爱情，而不是娃娃式的喜爱。

00:33:43.540 --> 00:34:07.900
<v Howie Chackowicz>So I had all these ideas about love. And of all the girls I knew, my theories were most intensely targeted at one girl, the most popular girl in school, Karen. She became my most serious crush. I carried a torch for Karen from grade 1 to grade 6. Though Karen didn't seem to like me much, one thing I'd learned about love on TV was that if one was sincere, love can break all boundaries.
<v Howie Chackowicz>所以我当时对爱情有各种各样的想法。在我认识的所有女孩中，我的理论最强烈地指向了一个女孩，也就是学校里最受欢迎的女孩——凯伦。她成了我最认真的暗恋对象。我从一年级到六年级一直对凯伦一往情深。尽管凯伦似乎并不怎么喜欢我，但我从电视上学到的一点关于爱情的知识是，如果一个人足够真诚，爱就能打破所有的界限。

00:34:07.900 --> 00:34:27.380
<v Howie Chackowicz>I believed that there would come a moment where I'd speak the words "I love you" to Karen with such tenderness and tears that it would break her heart, and she would cry too and confess her love. I would allow one brave tear to travel down my cheek.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我坚信会有这样一个时刻，我会带着无尽的温柔和眼泪对凯伦说出“我爱你”这三个字，这会让她心碎，她也会哭泣并向我表白。我会允许一滴勇敢的眼泪从我的脸颊滑落。

