<v Karen>乔纳森非常非常可爱。

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:55.680
<v Howie Chackowicz>Now, years later, I'm friends with Karen, actual friends with both her and her husband Alan. I even worked for him for a while. Karen and I have talked before about our elementary school days, usually steering the conversation towards how mean she was to me. But I've never really spoken with her about puppy love. I wanted to know what she remembered, whether she knew I even had a crush on her at all.
<v Howie Chackowicz>多年后的今天，我和凯伦成了朋友，真正意义上和她以及她的丈夫阿兰都成了朋友。我甚至还为他工作过一段时间。我和凯伦以前谈起过我们的小学时光，通常都会把话题引向她以前对我是多么苛刻。但我从来没有真正跟她聊过青梅竹马的纯情爱恋。我想知道她还记得什么，她是否知道我曾经暗恋过她。

00:34:55.680 --> 00:35:00.020
<v Howie Chackowicz>Who were your interests? We'll go year by year.
<v Howie Chackowicz>你当时对谁感兴趣？我们按年份来说。

00:35:00.020 --> 00:35:08.400
<v Karen>Keith, definitely, love interest. Barry Seller, the big one, the big one.
<v Karen>基思，绝对是，恋爱对象。巴里·塞勒，那个大人物，真正的大人物。

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:09.540
<v Howie Chackowicz>Lauren Wilter?
<v Howie Chackowicz>劳伦·威尔特？

00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:11.850
<v Karen>Yes, yes, yes.
<v Karen>对，对，对。

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:28.620
<v Howie Chackowicz>Notice who she doesn't mention. Even though it was so far in the past, a crush is still such an awkward thing to talk about. When I finally did tell her about how I felt about her when we were kids, I mumbled my way through it, backpedaling all over the place. I even forgot to actually point the mic at her.
<v Howie Chackowicz>注意她没有提到谁。尽管事情已经过去很久了，但谈论暗恋依然是一件很尴尬的事。当我最终告诉她我们小时候我对她的感觉时，我支支吾吾地说了半天，一直在语无伦次地打退堂鼓。我甚至忘了把麦克风对准她。

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:31.710
<v Howie Chackowicz>When I was in elementary school, you were a big crush.
<v Howie Chackowicz>当我上小学的时候，你是一个很大的暗恋对象。

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:32.420
<v Karen>Aw.
<v Karen>啊。

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:33.640
<v Howie Chackowicz>You see?
<v Howie Chackowicz>你看？

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:54.140
<v Karen>I didn't even know that. I thought I was just responsible for tormenting you. I didn't realize that there was a crush involved. Maybe at the time I knew, but I had no clue, actually, that you liked me, when I think back on it now.
<v Karen>我甚至都不知道。我以为我只负责折磨你。我其实根本没意识到其中还包含着暗恋。也许当时我知道，但现在回想起来，我真的毫无头绪你曾经喜欢过我。

00:35:54.140 --> 00:36:00.890
<v Howie Chackowicz>One time in the field, Keith told me to tackle you. He said if you tackle her, she'll like you, and then you'll be popular.
<v Howie Chackowicz>有一次在操场上，基思让我把你扑倒。他说如果你把她扑倒，她就会喜欢你，然后你就会受欢迎。

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:01.840
<v Karen>That is so funny.
<v Karen>太搞笑了。

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:02.710
<v Howie Chackowicz>This is the thing.
<v Howie Chackowicz>事情就是这样。

00:36:02.710 --> 00:36:05.430
<v Karen>You know what? That's very good advice, I think.
<v Karen>你知道吗？我觉得这是个很好的建议。

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:25.720
<v Howie Chackowicz>It's very bad advice. I don't want to break your leg. Basically, what happened was, I was standing off to the sidelines. I wasn't often picked to play. This was a coed game, and it seemed very fun. And Keith said, "I'm going to throw Karen the ball." And he you know he couldn't pronounce his R's. He goes, "You tackle her, Howard. Tackle her. Tackle her hard, and you'll be popular. And then everyone will like you.
<v Howie Chackowicz>这是个非常糟糕的建议。我可不想弄断你的腿。基本上，事情是这样的，当时我站在边上。我不太经常被选中上场。这是一场男女混合赛，看起来非常有趣。基思说：“我要把球传给凯伦。”你知道他发不清R的音。他说：“你扑倒她，霍华德。扑倒她。狠狠地扑倒她，你就会受欢迎。然后大家都会喜欢你。”

00:36:25.720 --> 00:36:42.160
<v Howie Chackowicz>I'm like, "OK, I'm going to do it." And I remember you were running, the sun was shining off, and your hair's bouncing, and you caught the ball, and I just-- I don't know what came over me. I just remember thinking that's what I had to do, was I had to tackle you. And I tackled you really hard.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我说：“好的，我要这么做。”我记得你在奔跑，阳光照耀着，你的头发在一颤一颤，你接住了球，然后我——我不知道自己怎么了。我只记得当时觉得这就是我必须要做的事，我必须把你扑倒。于是我狠狠地把你扑倒了。

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:48.390
<v Howie Chackowicz>You're on the ground and you're holding your leg. Now, any kind of logic would have dictated that that's not the way to get the girl you like.
<v Howie Chackowicz>你倒在地上，双手抱着腿。现在，任何一点逻辑都会表明，这根本不是追求喜欢的女孩的方法。

00:36:48.390 --> 00:37:01.210
<v Karen>Yeah,  but a lot of times the way young kids react or show affection is through physical-- like I was telling you before that I wrestled with Barry, because you just want to be close.
<v Karen>是的，但很多时候，小孩子的反应或表达喜爱的方式是通过肢体接触——就像我之前对你说的，我和巴里摔跤，因为你只是想亲近。

00:37:01.210 --> 00:37:21.780
<v Howie Chackowicz>This is not how she felt about it at the time. Because I felt the harder I tackled her, the more popular I'd be. I took her down like it was prison football. The game came to an immediate end, everyone circling Karen's writhing body, the football near her lay totally still. She was holding her leg, looking up at me saying, "You tub of lard, you broke my leg!"
<v Howie Chackowicz>她当时可不这么觉得。因为我觉得我扑得越狠，我就会越受欢迎。我像是在进行监狱橄榄球赛一样把你放倒了。比赛当即宣告结束，所有人都围着凯伦扭曲的身体，橄榄球就在她身边一动不动。她抱着腿，抬头看着我说：“你这个大肥猪，你弄断了我的腿！”

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:34.490
<v Howie Chackowicz>Karen doesn't remember any of this. She doesn't remember how she then jumped up, got four or five of her girlfriends in a huddle, and miraculously choreographed an impromptu chorus line of "Fatso Man" to the tune of the Village People's "Macho Man."
<v Howie Chackowicz>凯伦对这一切全无记忆。她不记得自己随后是如何一跃而起，把四五个女性朋友叫到一起窃窃私语，然后奇迹般地即兴编排了一段“胖子男”的合唱舞蹈，配乐是乡村乐队Village People的《肌肉男》。

00:37:34.490 --> 00:38:13.920
<v Howie Chackowicz>"Fatso, fatso man. I would not like to be a fatso man. Fatso, fatso man. I would not like to be a fatso!" And at that point, they all threw their hands up in the air in unison. I remember it so perfectly, but then after all, it was my crush. She had no recollection at the time the school photographer called her Daisy Duke and then turned around and called me Boss Hog, nor the fitness day that I beat her in a chariots of fire style race. She didn't even remember the biggest story of them all, our sixth grade graduation dance.
<v Howie Chackowicz>“胖子，胖子男。我才不想当个胖子男。胖子，胖子男。我才不想当个胖子！”就在那时，他们齐刷刷地把双手举向空中。我记得太清楚了，但毕竟，这是我的暗恋啊。她当时既不记得学校摄影师叫她黛西·杜克（Daisy Duke）然后转过身叫我“猪老板”（Boss Hog），也不记得在健身日那天我曾在《烈火战车》风格的赛跑中击败过她。她甚至不记得所有故事中最重磅的一个——我们的六年级毕业舞会。

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:15.950
<v Howie Chackowicz>Now, the last dance was "Stairway to Heaven."
<v Howie Chackowicz>现在，最后一支舞是《通往天堂的阶梯》。

00:38:15.950 --> 00:38:16.450
<v Karen>Mhm.
<v Karen>嗯哼。

00:38:16.380 --> 00:38:18.500
<v Howie Chackowicz>Right. Now, I went to the dance to dance with you.
<v Howie Chackowicz>对吧。当时我去舞会就是为了跟你跳舞。

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:19.230
<v Karen>Aw.
<v Karen>啊。

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:30.580
<v Howie Chackowicz>But I couldn't, because as I was walking, a line of people walked by and blocked me, and then there was a split second, but then you were in the arms of a grade seven.
<v Howie Chackowicz>但我没能跳成，因为当我走过去的时候，有一排人走过挡住了我，接着仅仅是一晃眼的工夫，你就已经在一个七年级学生的怀里了。

00:38:30.580 --> 00:38:32.170
<v Karen>Really? What was a grade seven doing in there?
<v Karen>真的吗？七年级的人怎么会混进那里？

00:38:32.170 --> 00:38:35.050
<v Howie Chackowicz>They crashed our grade six graduation dance.
<v Howie Chackowicz>他们大闹了我们的六年级毕业舞会。

00:38:35.050 --> 00:38:36.610
<v Karen>Who was it?
<v Karen>那是谁？

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:42.600
<v Howie Chackowicz>I don't know who he was. He was tall and thin. He had longish hair, and he came through the back door of the gym. You don't remember the last dance?
<v Howie Chackowicz>我不知道他是谁。他又高又瘦。留着长头发，他是从体育馆的后门进来的。你不记得最后一支舞了吗？

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:43.380
<v Karen>No.
<v Karen>不记得。

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:46.370
<v Howie Chackowicz>You kissed this gentleman.
<v Howie Chackowicz>你吻了这个绅士。

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:52.850
<v Karen>Did I? Like a peck or a make-out kiss? I don't think I was making out in grade six.
<v Karen>我有吗？是蜻蜓点水般的吻还是法式热吻？我觉得我在六年级时不会进行法式热吻。

00:38:52.850 --> 00:39:08.660
<v Howie Chackowicz>No, by adult standards, it was a peck. I'd say by grade six standards, you got laid.
<v Howie Chackowicz>不，按成年人的标准，那只是个轻吻。但按六年级的标准，你可真够开放的。

00:39:08.660 --> 00:39:11.750
<v Ira Glass>It turns out that Karen remembered exactly one story about me.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，凯伦对我的记忆里只精确地记得一件事。

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:52.720
<v Karen>Well, my most vivid memory of you is sitting in class, and the teacher asking us to pull out our homework, and you open up your desk and the paper overflowing out of that desk, and you rummaging frantically through the desk, trying to find what homework we were asked to take out and not being able to find it, and our teacher walking up to your desk, and everyone knowing what was coming, because it probably happened two days before, and the teacher just lacing into you, and dumping the contents of your desk on the floor.
<v Karen>好吧，我对你最深刻的记忆是坐在教室里，老师让我们拿出家庭作业，你打开你的课桌，纸张从那个课桌里溢了出来，你在课桌里疯狂地翻找，试图找出我们被要求拿出来的作业却怎么也找不到，然后我们的老师走到你的课桌前，每个人都知道接下来要发生什么，因为这可能两天前刚发生过，老师就开始狠狠地训斥你，并把你的课桌里的东西全都倒在地上了。

00:39:52.720 --> 00:39:57.500
<v Howie Chackowicz>Now, I mean, when that happened, did I seem cool, like a bad boy?
<v Howie Chackowicz>现在，我是说，当那件事发生的时候，我看起来很酷吗，就像个坏男孩？

00:39:57.500 --> 00:40:09.640
<v Karen>No, everyone felt very, very sad for you.
<v Karen>不，每个人都为你感到非常非常难过。

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:26.090
<v Howie Chackowicz>More than anything, I wanted Karen to notice me, but not in that way. I think the problem with my theories was that I expected her to fall for me the same way I fell for her, that she would see me from afar, reading to our classmates, sleeping like a little prince. I thought that's what it took for someone to fall in love.
<v Howie Chackowicz>比起什么都重要，我希望凯伦注意到我，但不是以那种方式。我想我的理论的问题在于，我期望她爱上我的方式和我爱上她的方式一样，她会远远地看着我，给同学们读书，像小王子一样安睡。我以为这正是一个人坠入爱河所需要的。

00:40:26.090 --> 00:40:47.120
<v Howie Chackowicz>I wanted her to think that this was the real me. I wanted to think it was the real me. And the truth of it was that the real real me was getting screamed at and having his desk spilled out on the ground each day.
<v Howie Chackowicz>我希望她认为这才是真正的我。我希望自己能认为这才是真正的我。但事实是，真正真实的我每天都在挨吼，课桌里的东西被倒在地上。

00:40:47.120 --> 00:41:14.600
<v Howie Chackowicz>There's a way you can love a girl in grade six that you'll never have again. There's something about kids, or at least the way I was as a kid, that is purely romantic, in the truest love sonneteering sense of the word. Only a year or two later, my theories on the ways love had changed drastically. By seventh grade, I had some spin the bottle sessions under my belt, and I had concluded that instead of dreaming about a true love I couldn't have, I should get a little bit more pragmatic about the whole thing.
<v Howie Chackowicz>人在六年级时对一个女孩的爱，是你余生再也体会不到的。小孩子身上有一种特质，或者说至少我小时候那种状态，是纯粹浪漫的，带有最真实的十四行诗意义上的爱情感。仅仅过了一二年，我对爱情看法的理论就发生了剧烈变化。到了七年级，我已经经历过好几次转瓶子游戏了，我得出结论：与其梦想着一个无法得到的真爱，不如对整件事变得更加现实一些。

00:41:14.600 --> 00:41:35.160
<v Howie Chackowicz>One night, after deciding I wanted to have a real girlfriend, I called up identical twin sisters I liked, Darlene and Elizabeth. Darlene answered. I told her that I liked her, and I asked her if she'd like to officially go out with me. She kindly told me that she only liked me as a friend but she was flattered. "No problem," I said. "Is Elizabeth home?"
<v Howie Chackowicz>一天晚上，在决定了我想拥有一个真正的女朋友后，我打电话给我喜欢的一对双胞胎姐妹，达琳和伊丽莎白。达琳接了电话。我告诉她我喜欢她，并问她是否愿意正式和我交往。她好心地告诉我，她只把我当朋友，不过她觉得很荣幸。“没问题，”我说。“伊丽莎白在家吗？”

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:53.380
<v Howie Chackowicz>She passed me over to her twin, who I made the same offer to. And Elizabeth said "sure," and that was it. "They're identical twins. What's the difference," I figured. We went out for two whole months. It was great.
<v Howie Chackowicz>她把电话转给了她的双胞胎妹妹，我向她提出了同样的请求。伊丽莎白说“行”，事情就这么定了。“她们是双胞胎。有什么区别，”我想。我们交往了整整两个月。感觉太棒了。

00:41:53.380 --> 00:42:41.740
<v Ira Glass>Howie Chackowicz. He's the creator, writer, and artist behind Howie Action Comics.
<v Ira Glass>豪伊·查科维茨。他是“豪伊动作漫画”的创作者、编剧和画师。

00:42:41.740 --> 00:43:02.520
<v Ira Glass>Four, "When Small Thoughts Meet Big Brains." OK, so all this hour we've been talking about kid logic. And sometimes, the incorrect logic of childhood does not get corrected during childhood. It does not get corrected till much, much later, when childhood is long over. Alex Blumberg explains.
<v Ira Glass>第四部分，“当小思想遇到大脑袋”。好的，这一整小时我们都在谈论儿童逻辑。有时候，童年时期不正确的逻辑并不会在童年中得到纠正。它直到很久很久以后，当童年早已结束时才会被纠正。亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）为你解释。

00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:21.360
<v Alex Blumberg>I can reconstruct the events that led me to one of the most embarrassing conversations of my adult life. The chain starts back when I was 11 or 12, when I first heard the term Nielsen family. I was probably listening to some adults talk. And from their conversation I gathered that networks consulted Nielsen families to find out how popular a television show was.
<v Alex Blumberg>我可以重构那些导致了我成年后最尴尬对话之一的事件。这条链条要追溯到我11或12岁的时候，当时我第一次听到“尼尔森家庭”这个词。我当时可能在听一些成年人聊天。从他们的谈话中，我了解到各大电视网会向尼尔森家庭咨询，以了解某个电视节目的受欢迎程度。

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:44.140
<v Alex Blumberg>But that didn't make sense. Why would they only ask people named Nielsen which shows they liked? I started thinking. I knew that when they figured things like this out, they didn't ask everybody. They just asked a small percentage of people and then extrapolated. I think I figured they'd done some research and found that the name Nielsen, because it was a common name maybe, and it seemed to cut across class and economic lines, actually came pretty close to a representative sample.
<v Alex Blumberg>但这说不通。为什么他们只询问那些姓尼尔森的人喜欢什么节目？我开始思考。我知道当他们计算这类事情时，他们不会询问所有人。他们只询问一小部分人，然后进行外推。我想我当时觉得他们做过一些研究，发现“尼尔森”这个名字，也许因为它是一个常见的名字，而且它似乎跨越了阶层和经济界限，实际上非常接近一个有代表性的样本。

00:43:44.140 --> 00:44:03.030
<v Alex Blumberg>I knew this wasn't the way they measured public opinion now, but it seemed like the Nielsen surveys had been around for a while, and I figured they were just a holdover from a more primitive, less statistically rigorous time. After that, I really didn't think about it again. Or if I did, it was only with a mild curiosity. "I wonder why TV still does it that way?"
<v Alex Blumberg>我知道这不是他们现在衡量公众舆论的方式，但看起来尼尔森调查已经存在了一段时间，我以为这只是一个更原始、统计学上不太严谨的时代的遗留产物。在那之后，我真的没再想过这件事。或者如果我想过，也只是带着淡淡的好奇心。“我纳闷为什么电视现在还要用那种方式？”

00:44:03.030 --> 00:44:27.070
<v Alex Blumberg>Fast forward 20 years. I was talking with a friend of mine, who was telling me about her friend, who had been selected to be a Nielsen family. And I said to her, "Isn't it weird that they're all named Nielsen?" My friend looked at me for what seemed like a long time. Somewhere during her very long pause, because of the very long pause, in fact, I realized, "Of course they're not all named Nielsen. That makes no sense at all."
<v Alex Blumberg>时间快进20年。我和一个朋友聊天，她跟我提起她的一个朋友被选中成为了一个尼尔森家庭。我对她说：“他们全都姓尼尔森，这不是很奇怪吗？”我朋友看着我，那眼神感觉持续了很久。在她那漫长的停顿期间，事实上正是因为那漫长的停顿，我意识到：“他们当然不可能都姓尼尔森。这根本毫无道理。”

00:44:27.070 --> 00:44:37.750
<v Alex Blumberg>At the time of this conversation, I was 34 years old, and I couldn't believe I'd gotten this far without ever stopping to think it through. It made me wonder what else I'd missed, and if this has ever happen to anyone besides me.
<v Alex Blumberg>在这次对话时，我34岁了，我简直不敢相信自己竟然活到了这个岁数，却从来没有停下来仔细思考过这件事。这让我好奇自己还错过过什么，以及这种情况是否也发生在别人身上。

00:44:37.750 --> 00:44:56.820
<v Jodi Mace>When I was a kid, and I would see the school crossing signs, and there's a picture of the little kids walking, and then it would say "School Xing" And I thought that the "Xing" was a word. And I pronounced it "zing."
<v Jodi Mace>当我还是个孩子的时候，我会看到学校人行横道标志，上面画着小孩子走路的图案，然后上面写着“School Xing”（学校人行横道）。我以为“Xing”是一个单词。我把它发音读成“zing”。

00:44:56.820 --> 00:45:12.860
<v Alex Blumberg>Turns out I'm not alone. I've been talking to people about this for weeks, and there's a lot of us out there, like me and this woman Jodi Mace, carting around our childhood beliefs well into adulthood. Jodi thought there were lots of xings, deer xings, railroad xings. It makes sense.
<v Alex Blumberg>事实证明我并不孤单。我为这事和人们聊了好几个星期，像我以及这位名叫乔迪·麦斯（Jodi Mace）的女士这样的人有很多，我们把童年时期的错误认知一直带到了成年。乔迪以为有很多“xing”，鹿行横道（deer xings）、铁路行横道（railroad xings）。这倒也说得通。

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:57.680
<v Jodi Mace>Well, I was in my 20s, and I was walking into work, and about 10 geese walked in front of me on the sidewalk. And so I just turned to my coworker and casually said, "It looks like they should have a xing sign there for the geese." There was a long, awkward silence. And I thought that he was thinking, "You know, that really is a good idea." But instead, he finally said, "You know, 'xing' isn't in a word."
<v Jodi Mace>嗯，我当时20多岁，正走在上班的路上，大约10只大雁在人行道上从我面前走过。于是我转过身随口对同事说：“看起来他们应该在那里为大雁立一个xing标志。”空气中陷入了一阵漫长而尴尬的沉默。我以为他当时在想：“你知道，这确实是个好主意。”但相反，他最后却说：“你知道的，‘xing’根本不是一个词。”

00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:08.220
<v Alex Blumberg>In talking to people, I found out that a lot of these lingering misconceptions involve mispronunciation. And often the mispronunciation survives into adulthood because the mistake just sounds better. It makes more sense.
<v Alex Blumberg>在与人们交谈的过程中，我发现许多这种挥之不去的误解都涉及错误发音。而且通常这些错误发音能延续到成年期，是因为这个错误听起来更好。它感觉更合乎逻辑。

00:46:08.220 --> 00:46:22.200
<v Jodi Mace>You know, it should be a word. And it should be xing. You don't want a kid to walk slowly across the crossing. If he's smart, he's going to xing.
<v Jodi Mace>你知道的，它本该是个词。而且它就该是xing。你可不想让一个孩子慢慢地走过人行横道。如果他够聪明，他就会xing。

00:46:22.200 --> 00:46:48.980
<v Alex Blumberg>Consider the word "misled." I talked to three people, including my own father, who used to pronounce it "my-zulled." All three believed it was the past tense of a nonexistent verb "misle," which means "to deceive" or "to mislead." There's another guy I spoke to who thought well in his early 20s that the word "quesadilla" was Spanish for "What's the deal?"
<v Alex Blumberg>想想“misled”这个词。我跟三个人聊过，包括我亲爹，他以前把这个词读成“my-zulled”。他们三个都坚信这是一个不存在的动词“misle”的过去式，意思是“欺骗”或“误导”。我还跟另一个人聊过，他在二十岁出头时，以为“quesadilla（墨西哥馅饼）”在西班牙语里的意思是“有什么事吗？”

00:46:48.980 --> 00:47:02.300
<v Alex Blumberg>Most of the common childhood myths, like that babies come from storks, get corrected sooner or later. They're not obscure enough to sneak into adulthood unscrutinized. But occasionally even a very popular childhood myth can make it through, like unicorns.
<v Alex Blumberg>绝大多数常见的童年神话，比如婴儿是鹳鸟送来的，迟早都会被纠正。它们不够冷门，无法在成年后逃过审查。但有时，哪怕是非常受欢迎的童年神话也能蒙混过关，比如独角兽。

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:06.470
<v Christy Kruger>In my head, a unicorn wasn't really any different than a zebra.
<v Christy Kruger>在我脑海里，独角兽其实跟斑马没什么区别。

00:47:06.470 --> 00:47:07.850
<v Alex Blumberg>This is Christy Kruger.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是克里斯蒂·克鲁格（Christy Kruger）。

00:47:07.850 --> 00:47:14.920
<v Christy Kruger>I mean, in terms of believability, I think the unicorn's really ahead of the dinosaur.
<v Christy Kruger>我的意思是，就可信度而言，我觉得独角兽真的比恐龙更让人相信。

00:47:14.920 --> 00:47:16.730
<v Alex Blumberg>What do you mean?
<v Alex Blumberg>你这话什么意思？

00:47:16.730 --> 00:47:36.490
<v Christy Kruger>Well, I mean when you think about a dinosaur, like, from a kid's perspective, a dinosaur is one of these really large, monstrous animals roaming the earth. And then you have a unicorn, which is basically just a horse with a horn.
<v Christy Kruger>嗯，我的意思是，当你想到恐龙时，比如说，从孩子的角度来看，恐龙是那种在地球上游荡的、非常巨大、像怪物一样的动物。然后你再看独角兽，它基本上就是一匹长了角的马。

00:47:36.490 --> 00:47:48.800
<v Alex Blumberg>As Christy Kruger grew up, she says that if she ever thought about unicorns, they were on a grassy plain, somewhere in Africa, drinking from a watering hole with the wildebeest and the impala. And then one night, she found herself in a conversation at a party.
<v Alex Blumberg>克里斯蒂·克鲁格长大后，她说如果她曾经想起独角兽，它们应该是在一片长满青草的平原上，在非洲的某个地方，和角马、黑斑羚一起在水坑边喝水。然后有一天晚上，她在派对上和人聊起天来。

00:47:48.800 --> 00:48:15.710
<v Christy Kruger>It was about a group of five to seven people, standing around the keg, just talking. And somehow, a discussion of endangered species came up, in which I posed the question "Is the unicorn endangered or extinct?" And basically, there was a big gap of silence.
<v Christy Kruger>那是大约五到七个人围着啤酒桶站着聊天。不知怎么的，聊到了濒危物种的话题，于是我提出了这样一个问题：“独角兽是濒临灭绝还是已经灭绝了？”基本上，现场陷入了一片长久的沉默。

00:48:15.710 --> 00:48:20.210
<v Alex Blumberg>As you might be gathering, at some point in all these stories, you come to a big gap of silence.
<v Alex Blumberg>正如你可能已经感受到的，所有这些故事讲到某个时刻，都会出现一段长久的沉默。

00:48:20.210 --> 00:48:46.640
<v Christy Kruger>And then everybody laughed. And then that laughter was followed by more silence when they realized I wasn't laughing. And I was like, "Yeah. I-- oh god. Unicorns aren't real? Oh. Oh no!"
<v Christy Kruger>然后大家都笑了。接着那笑声后面又是更多的沉默，因为他们意识到我没笑。我说：“对啊。我——哦天哪。独角兽不是真的吗？哦。哦不！”

00:48:46.640 --> 00:49:05.720
<v Alex Blumberg>Sometimes, a ridiculous belief will survive into adulthood, and it's our parents who are to blame. Robin didn't think there was anything strange about the way she was raised. She lived together with her sister and her parents in a nice house in the suburbs. She went to school like the other kids, watched TV and did her homework. And she ate the exact same thing for dinner every night of her life-- baked chicken.
<v Alex Blumberg>有时候，荒谬的信念会一直延续到成年，而这得怪我们的父母。罗宾（Robin）并不觉得自己的成长方式有什么奇怪的。她和姐姐还有父母一起住在郊区的一栋漂亮房子里。她像其他孩子一样上学，看电视，做作业。而且她这辈子每天晚上的晚饭都吃一模一样的东西——烤鸡。

00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:28.530
<v Robin>It was like Monday, chicken, Tuesday, chicken, Wednesday, chicken, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, chicken. Chicken, chicken, chicken, chicken every night of my life until I left for college.
<v Robin>就像周一吃鸡肉，周二吃鸡肉，周三吃鸡肉，周四、周五、周六、周日，吃鸡肉。在我离家上大学之前，我一辈子每天晚上都在吃鸡肉、鸡肉、鸡肉、鸡肉。

00:49:28.530 --> 00:49:49.210
<v Robin>At the end of the first week of college, when everyone's desperately trying to fit in and it's important that you act cool, and sophisticated, and whatever, everyone begins complaining about the food that we're being served. What was the hard stuff in the sloppy Joe? What was that mystery meat? What animal did it come from?
<v Robin>上大学的第一周结束时，当每个人都在拼命融入集体，并且表现得酷炫、成熟等等显得尤为重要时，大家开始抱怨学校供应的饭菜。三明治里的硬块到底是什么？那种神秘的肉是什么？它是从什么动物身上来的？

00:49:49.210 --> 00:50:13.850
<v Robin>And I'm looking at these people like they are crazy. The variety we were getting here, every night, every night there's a different meal. I mean, one night it's mac and cheese, one night it's mystery meat, one night it's sloppy Joe. One night it's-- I was like, how can you critic-- I mean, it's a testament to what great chefs they must be that they can make a different meal every single night of the week.
<v Robin>而我看着这些人，就像看疯子一样。我们在这里得到的品种，每天晚上，每天晚上都是不同的饭菜。我的意思是，今晚是通心粉加奶酪，明晚是神秘肉，后晚是三明治。有一晚是——我当时想，你怎么能挑剔——我的意思是，这证明了他们一定是很棒的厨师，因为他们能在每个星期的每一天都做出不同的饭菜。

00:50:13.850 --> 00:50:52.240
<v Robin>And they just kind of-- they kind of stared, and they're like "What?" And I'm like, "What what?" What's running through my head is, wait a minute. These people are implying that they had variation in their meal plan for their entire life. It's mind-bending. I mean, I don't care what I learned throughout college. This is the revelation that has stuck with me. This is what I've learned. All of a sudden, like holy god!
<v Robin>然后他们就有点——他们有点瞪着眼睛，然后说：“什么？”我说：“什么什么？”我脑子里转的念头是，等一下。这些人是在暗示他们一辈子都在吃各种各样的饭菜吗？这太颠覆三观了。我的意思是，我才不管我在大学里学到了什么呢。这就是一直烙印在我脑海里的启示。这就是我学到的东西。突然之间，就像天哪！

00:50:52.240 --> 00:51:20.130
<v Alex Blumberg>When Robin came home for Thanksgiving that year, she confronted her mother with the startling fact that everyone else ate things besides chicken growing up. Her mother just shrugged her shoulders and said, "You liked chicken." Robin had to concede the point. Even when they went out to restaurants, Robin ordered chicken. They all had.
<v Alex Blumberg>那年感恩节罗宾回家时，她向母亲抛出了一个惊人的事实：除了鸡肉，其他人从小到大还吃过别的东西。她母亲只是耸了耸肩，说：“你喜欢吃鸡肉。”罗宾不得不承认这一点。甚至当他们去餐馆时，罗宾点的也是鸡肉。他们全家都这样。

00:51:20.130 --> 00:51:39.870
<v Alex Blumberg>Here's one more. When Harriet Lerner was a girl, her family was going through some lean years. There were two kids. The house needed repairs. There wasn't much money for holiday gifts. Harriet was seven, and she wanted a bike. Her sister, Susan, was 12. She wanted a set of encyclopedias. But when they came downstairs on Christmas morning, there were only two small boxes waiting for them.
<v Alex Blumberg>还有一个。哈丽特·勒纳（Harriet Lerner）小时候，家里正经历一段拮据的日子。家里有两个孩子。房子需要修缮。节日礼物的钱不多。哈丽特七岁，她想要一辆自行车。她姐姐苏珊（Susan）12岁。她想要一套百科全书。但当他们在圣诞节早晨下楼时，只有两个小盒子在等着他们。

00:51:39.870 --> 00:52:09.510
<v Harriet Lerner>What was inside them-- and we both had exactly the same gift-- were these real ugly metal tissue holders painted black with these corny red and yellow roses. They were painted with these cheesy looking red and yellow roses. And I looked at my tissue box and I started to cry.
<v Harriet Lerner>里面装的东西——而且我们俩收到的礼物一模一样——是那种真正丑陋的金属纸巾盒，漆成黑色，上面画着俗气的红黄相间的玫瑰。它们画着那种看起来很廉价的红黄相间的玫瑰。我看着自己的纸巾盒，开始哭了起来。

00:52:09.510 --> 00:52:31.350
<v Harriet Lerner>And I looked at my big sister, Susan, and I thought, of course, she was going to cry too. And she looked like maybe she was going to cry, but then she put on a big smile. And then she told me that the boxes were painted by trained monkeys.
<v Harriet Lerner>我看着我的大姐姐苏珊，心想，她肯定也会哭的。她看起来好像也快哭了，但随后她露出了灿烂的笑容。接着她告诉我，这些盒子是由受过训练的猴子画的。

00:52:31.350 --> 00:52:50.100
<v Alex Blumberg>The box became Harriet's prized possession. She kept it on display in her room through elementary school, through high school. Her friends asked her about it and she'd say, "Oh yeah. It was painted by trained monkeys." Nobody ever challenged her on it. Maybe because she believed it herself so completely. And then one day, she was home from college, back in the house where she grew up.
<v Alex Blumberg>这个盒子成了哈丽特最珍贵的宝贝。整个小学和中学期间，她一直把它摆在自己的房间里。她的朋友们问起这事，她就会说：“哦，对啊。这是受过训练的猴子画的。”从来没有人对此提出过质疑。也许是因为她自己也深信不疑。然后有一天，她从大学回家，回到了她长大的房子里。

00:52:50.100 --> 00:53:13.080
<v Harriet Lerner>And I'm going through some papers, or maybe I was snooping through Susan's papers. And I found a composition, and it had her name on it. And she had written it in high school, and it was called the Tissue Box Story. So I sat down on the floor of Susan's bedroom to read this composition.
<v Harriet Lerner>我在翻找一些文件，或者我可能是在翻看苏珊的文件。我发现了一篇作文，上面写着她的名字。那是她在高中时写的，题目叫《纸巾盒的故事》。于是我在苏珊卧室的地板上坐了下来，开始读这篇作文。

00:53:13.080 --> 00:53:42.300
<v Harriet Lerner>And Susan told the story just as I told it, except that she wrote how she felt when she saw me crying, and how she then looked at my parents and saw that my mother was about to cry too, and how she looked at the tissue boxes and then she remembered that my father had a friend who made them, and she knew how much my parents hated taking charity.
<v Harriet Lerner>苏珊讲述这个故事的经过和我讲的一模一样，只是她写了她看到我哭时的感受，然后她看了看我的父母，发现我母亲也快哭了，接着她看了看纸巾盒，然后她想起我父亲有个朋友制作了这些盒子，她知道我父母有多么讨厌接受别人的救济。

00:53:42.300 --> 00:54:15.060
<v Harriet Lerner>And suddenly, even though she was about to cry, she forced herself to smile, and she pretended those boxes were painted by trained monkeys. And then, of course, I didn't know any of this. But the funny thing she wrote in her composition is that she just rushed upstairs and started crying all over her pillow.
<v Harriet Lerner>突然之间，尽管她自己也快哭了，她还是强迫自己微笑，并假装这些盒子是由受过训练的猴子画的。然后，当然，我对这一切一无所知。但她在作文里写了一件有趣的事，那就是她马上跑上楼，把脸埋在枕头上大哭了一场。

00:54:15.060 --> 00:54:39.080
<v Harriet Lerner>And she wasn't really sad about the gift, really, is what she said in the composition. She wasn't sure why she was crying except that it was like she had volunteered to be a grown up before she was even ready for it.
<v Harriet Lerner>正如她在作文里所说的那样，她其实并不是为礼物本身感到难过。她不确定自己为什么在哭，只是感觉自己好像还没准备好，就主动要求成为一个成年人。

00:54:39.080 --> 00:54:58.880
<v Harriet Lerner>Up until that moment, I had never thought to question my sister's story. I had never subjected it to the scrutiny of a grown up mind. I mean, I was 20. I don't know. I had this tissue box that was painted by trained monkeys, and then it wasn't painted by trained monkeys, really.
<v Harriet Lerner>直到那一刻之前，我从未想过要质疑我姐姐的故事。我从未用成年人的思维去审视它。我的意思是，我当时20岁了。我不知道。我曾经拥有一个由受过训练的猴子画的纸巾盒，然后发现它其实并不是由受过训练的猴子画的。

00:54:58.880 --> 00:55:21.430
<v Alex Blumberg>Up until reading that story, Harriet thought that her sister's lies had been only to torment her, like the time Harriet swallowed an apple seed and her big sister convinced her that she had an apple tree growing inside her. She'd always been jealous of her sister, always wanted to be the big sister. But reading her sister's story that day made her realize how responsible her sister felt for her, and for their entire family, and how there were benefits to being the baby.
<v Alex Blumberg>在读到那个故事之前，哈丽特一直以为她姐姐的谎言只是为了折磨她，就像有一次哈丽特吞下了一颗苹果核，她大姐姐说服她，说她体内会长出一棵苹果树一样。她一直嫉妒姐姐，一直想当大姐姐。但那天读到姐姐的故事，让她意识到姐姐为她、为整个家庭承担了多少责任，也让她明白了当幼小的孩子有什么好处。

00:55:21.430 --> 00:55:28.580
<v Alex Blumberg>It was good to learn all that. But the vision of the lie, that we live in a world where monkeys can be trained to paint, is hard to give up.
<v Alex Blumberg>能了解到这一切挺好的。但是那个谎言所构筑的愿景——我们生活在一个可以训练猴子画画的世界里——是很难放下的。

00:55:28.580 --> 00:55:40.730
<v Harriet Lerner>And really, it's just that I can still picture this tissue box and how much I loved it, this tissue box painted by trained monkeys.
<v Harriet Lerner>说真的，只是因为我依然能想象出这个纸巾盒以及我是多么喜爱它，这个由受过训练的猴子画的纸巾盒。

00:55:40.730 --> 00:55:58.530
<v Alex Blumberg>I know what she means. For me, there's something appealingly weird about a world where only people who happen to have been born with the name Nielsen get to decide what goes on television. And not long after the day that Jodi Mace's coworker set her straight about the word "xing," she found herself on the opposite side of the exact same situation.
<v Alex Blumberg>我懂她的意思。对某些人来说，一个只有碰巧生下来姓尼尔森（Nielsen）的人才能决定电视播放内容的世界，有一种吸引人的诡异感。就在乔迪·梅斯（Jodi Mace）的同事向她纠正“xing”这个词的不久之后，她发现自己遇到了完全相同的另一面。

00:55:58.530 --> 00:56:21.150
<v Alex Blumberg>She was having a conversation with another coworker, and he asked her if elves were real. "Elves? Like that live in the forest, with the pointy toes?" He nodded. She paused, and then she said, "Yeah, of course they are."
<v Alex Blumberg>她当时正在和另一个同事聊天，同事问她精灵是不是真的。“精灵？就像那种住在森林里、脚趾尖尖的精灵？”他点了点头。她停顿了一下，然后说：“对啊，当然是真的。”

00:56:21.150 --> 00:56:34.440
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg. In his day job, he makes podcasts, great podcasts, Reply All, Heavyweight, StartUp , lots of other ones, at gimletmedia.com.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）。他的日常工作是制作播客，很棒的播客，比如《Reply All》、《Heavyweight》、《StartUp》以及其他许多播客，网址是 gimletmedia.com。

